{
  "chapter.3B831E99D9522F01.title": "09 • ComputerCraft: Useful Little Programs",
  "task.3DAEB60AEC2732EC.title": "I read and understood this guide",
  "quest.1FCC68D3F7711256.title": "Start here: ComputerCraft",
  "quest.1FCC68D3F7711256.quest_subtitle": "Guide and tips",
  "quest.1FCC68D3F7711256.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "CC:Tweaked adds programmable computers, monitors and turtles using Lua. Advanced Peripherals connects those programs to more modded systems, including storage networks and colonies. Start with small programs you understand, then use them to observe or automate a specific job.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Your route: terminal and editor -> Lua basics -> peripherals -> monitor -> inventory reports -> turtle movement -> Advanced Peripherals -> a safe, useful dashboard.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Read the next guide card before building. This chapter teaches working setups; it does not replace JEI recipes or the mod's own manual."
  ],
  "task.79FA27CEAAFCD995.title": "I read and understood this guide",
  "quest.0B3760DF95BD695E.title": "How to use this guide",
  "quest.0B3760DF95BD695E.quest_subtitle": "Guide and tips",
  "quest.0B3760DF95BD695E.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Arrows suggest a learning order. Item tasks check what you carry; a checkmark is your own confirmation of a practical step. Neither is proof that an entire machine or colony works.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Open a quest, read Why / Do / Tip, then click its item to inspect it in JEI. R shows recipes and U shows uses with the default bindings. Read ahead, complete the described experiment, and only then tick practical tasks. Items are not consumed.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Use JEI for this pack's quantities and substitutions. Pick a small test area and keep outputs visible. Existing equipment can count; briefly pick it up only when safe. Troubleshooting and a final project are at the end."
  ],
  "task.224217FDF8B76876.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.303B9B71E3EB0278.title": "Your first terminal",
  "quest.303B9B71E3EB0278.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.303B9B71E3EB0278.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A computer gives you a shell for commands and an editor for Lua programs. The advanced model adds a color display and mouse support.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Place the computer, open it and run help, then programs to see installed commands. Run edit hello.lua, enter the example, save and exit through the editor menu, then run hello.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "print(\"Hello, workshop!\")",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "The editor menu is reached with Ctrl. Save changes before leaving; the shell runs the saved file, not unsaved text in the editor."
  ],
  "task.34C3D1910D193172.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.1C4CD738E2979024.title": "Values and a small calculation",
  "quest.1C4CD738E2979024.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.1C4CD738E2979024.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Lua variables store values and functions perform actions. A tiny calculation is easier to debug than a complete factory controller.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Create supplies.lua using the example below. Change stacks and rerun it. Add another print line that explains the unit so you can interpret the result later.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "local stacks = 3",
    "local items = stacks * 64",
    "print(\"Items in full stacks: \" .. items)",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Quoted text is a string; an unquoted number can be used in arithmetic. Read an error's filename and line number before changing unrelated code."
  ],
  "task.6C1A764386CA70DC.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.258BFAAC1F8A3AF4.title": "Find connected peripherals",
  "quest.258BFAAC1F8A3AF4.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.258BFAAC1F8A3AF4.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Peripherals are devices a computer can call. A local side such as left or a wired network name identifies a specific device.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Attach a monitor or another supported peripheral and run peripherals. Then run the example to list the names and types visible to this computer.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "for _, name in ipairs(peripheral.getNames()) do",
    "  print(name, peripheral.getType(name))",
    "end",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "A device on another cable network will not appear just because it is nearby. Use discovered names rather than inventing a device address."
  ],
  "task.3C230F2A19704FD7.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.4E1E39A846877C4D.title": "A monitor that says something useful",
  "quest.4E1E39A846877C4D.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.4E1E39A846877C4D.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A monitor displays a program's output where you can see it without opening the computer.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Connect an Advanced Monitor and run the example. Extend the screen with matching monitor blocks if desired, then adjust the text scale and layout only after the message appears.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "local m = peripheral.find(\"monitor\")",
    "assert(m, \"Connect a monitor first\")",
    "m.clear()",
    "m.setCursorPos(1, 1)",
    "m.write(\"Workshop online\")",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "peripheral.find returns a matching device; with several monitors, use peripheral.wrap and a specific discovered name for predictable placement."
  ],
  "task.18A24BFA29B900BE.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.67B332F525EB45D6.title": "Reach devices with a wired network",
  "quest.67B332F525EB45D6.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.67B332F525EB45D6.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Wired modems and networking cable make distant peripherals accessible to one computer.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Attach wired modems to the computer and a supported inventory or monitor, connect them with cable, and activate peripheral sharing on the relevant modem. Run peripherals again.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Record the name printed when a peripheral is made available. A cable connection and an exposed peripheral are separate steps."
  ],
  "task.06CE76D01E6AC122.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.2E8ED2C1830C784A.title": "Inspect inventory without moving it",
  "quest.2E8ED2C1830C784A.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.2E8ED2C1830C784A.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Read-only reports are a useful first automation project because you can learn the API without rearranging storage.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Expose a chest through the wired network and run the example. Compare the reported slots, item identifiers and counts with the chest contents.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "local inv = peripheral.find(\"inventory\")",
    "assert(inv, \"Expose a chest on the wired network\")",
    "for slot, item in pairs(inv.list()) do",
    "  print(slot, item.name, item.count)",
    "end",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "An inventory's list is indexed by occupied slots, so pairs is appropriate. An empty chest can legitimately print nothing."
  ],
  "task.297F2EC8ABB23A87.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.122E9F59D4910A3F.title": "Discover methods before calling them",
  "quest.122E9F59D4910A3F.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.122E9F59D4910A3F.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Peripherals expose different functions. A storage bridge and a vanilla chest are not interchangeable APIs.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Choose a name from peripherals and substitute it for the placeholder below. Inspect the returned method names, then consult that peripheral's documentation before calling a method with arguments.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "local name = \"REPLACE_WITH_PERIPHERAL_NAME\"",
    "assert(peripheral.isPresent(name), \"Check the peripheral name\")",
    "textutils.pagedTabulate(peripheral.getMethods(name))",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Method discovery tells you names, not parameter meanings. Begin with information queries before trying export, craft or movement methods."
  ],
  "task.2460A696984617BD.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.151179D43D671F67.title": "Repeat politely",
  "quest.151179D43D671F67.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.151179D43D671F67.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A repeating program must yield so the computer can handle events. Unbounded work without pauses can be terminated as too long without yielding.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Run this bounded five-step loop, then adapt it to refresh a read-only value. Stop a running program with Ctrl+T when you need to regain the shell.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "for step = 1, 5 do",
    "  print(\"Refresh \" .. step)",
    "  sleep(1)",
    "end",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Start with a finite loop while debugging. A one-second refresh is usually plenty for a status display."
  ],
  "task.250644FE506FD40C.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.2A14FE93C40B676D.title": "Keep settings in a file",
  "quest.2A14FE93C40B676D.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.2A14FE93C40B676D.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Separating device names and thresholds from program logic makes a setup easier to maintain.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Copy your working program before editing it. Use the built-in help for settings and files, then store one device name or threshold as a setting and read it back.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Use list, copy and edit to inspect and preserve your files. Back up working code before making it run automatically."
  ],
  "task.18928A6904CC79B0.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.2FDF6DC22F117D17.title": "Meet the turtle",
  "quest.2FDF6DC22F117D17.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.2FDF6DC22F117D17.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A turtle is a mobile computer with an inventory. Movement uses fuel unless the server configuration makes fuel unlimited.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Place it in a clear test area, give it a label with label set GuideTurtle, inspect its inventory and use the refuel program with suitable fuel. Run the example to attempt one forward move.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "print(\"Fuel:\", turtle.getFuelLevel())",
    "local ok, reason = turtle.forward()",
    "print(ok, reason)",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "A failed move returns a reason; handle it rather than assuming movement succeeded. Keep a labeled starting position and avoid edges while testing."
  ],
  "task.2D89A67748656405.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.70858E0F111B76CE.title": "Inspect before digging",
  "quest.70858E0F111B76CE.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.70858E0F111B76CE.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A turtle program should know what is ahead before it breaks blocks. A failed action must not silently corrupt its idea of its position.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Place a disposable block in front of the turtle and run turtle.inspect in a small program. Print the returned block name when inspection succeeds, then try with empty space.",
    "",
    "&b&lEXAMPLE&r",
    "local found, block = turtle.inspect()",
    "if found then",
    "  print(block.name)",
    "else",
    "  print(\"No block in front\")",
    "end",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Keep mining experiments outside your base. Never turn an untested movement loop into an unattended quarry."
  ],
  "task.73FB2D05A5FD219F.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.36913CEC37E1C97E.title": "A bounded turtle job",
  "quest.36913CEC37E1C97E.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.36913CEC37E1C97E.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Useful automation needs a stopping condition, fuel awareness and a plan for full inventory or blocked movement.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Write a three-step movement experiment that stops immediately on a failed move and prints the reason. Run it in a clear marked lane, then intentionally block the lane and test again.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Only add digging, placement or harvesting after movement and failure handling work. Keep the first job small enough to inspect by eye."
  ],
  "task.099E1F8F1F4D586E.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.707FD598FC96C2A2.title": "Connect Refined Storage",
  "quest.707FD598FC96C2A2.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.707FD598FC96C2A2.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "The RS Bridge exposes supported Refined Storage information and operations to a computer. The storage network still needs its ordinary power and connections.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Connect the bridge to your working RS network and computer. Discover its peripheral name and methods, then use the installed-version documentation to query a small piece of network information.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Begin read-only. Export and crafting methods can consume resources or request large jobs; add explicit quantities and limits only after you understand their arguments."
  ],
  "task.7AF2F0EBABB4BA20.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.4F8565EDD613BEBF.title": "Observe your colony",
  "quest.4F8565EDD613BEBF.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.4F8565EDD613BEBF.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "The Colony Integrator makes supported MineColonies information available to a computer, which can turn it into a useful local status report.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Place the integrator where it can access your colony, attach it to the computer and discover its methods. Choose a documented read-only query such as basic colony information or requests, then display a short summary.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Check ownership/permissions and colony access if the peripheral exists but returns no useful colony data. Integration does not replace the colony's own request system."
  ],
  "task.42CC5BDC884475EF.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.44C89EC8B31860C1.title": "Measure the factory",
  "quest.44C89EC8B31860C1.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.44C89EC8B31860C1.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Advanced Peripherals adds detectors for observing systems beyond ordinary inventories. A useful measurement tells you what action to take.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Read the Energy Detector documentation and place it in a suitable energy path. Discover the installed methods, query a supported measurement and compare idle with running machinery.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Distinguish throughput from stored energy. A cable detector and a battery report may measure different things; label the number and its unit."
  ],
  "task.0FE204EB46ED7965.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.434A579A370CCA1B.title": "Start a dashboard automatically",
  "quest.434A579A370CCA1B.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.434A579A370CCA1B.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A startup program runs when the computer boots. It is convenient only after the program handles missing devices and ordinary failures.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Save a proven read-only dashboard as startup.lua. Reboot the computer, confirm it starts, then disconnect its monitor and make the program report a useful error rather than repeatedly attempting world-changing actions.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Keep a normal named backup of the program. Startup timing can differ from device availability, so plan for reconnects or a short delayed retry."
  ],
  "task.6960CF6F7C20CD66.title": "I read and understood this guide",
  "quest.234305EAD3616597.title": "Troubleshoot by layers",
  "quest.234305EAD3616597.quest_subtitle": "Guide and tips",
  "quest.234305EAD3616597.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "Syntax errors, missing peripherals, unsupported methods and incorrect arguments are different failure classes.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Read the error and line number, check the device appears in peripherals, inspect its methods, then confirm arguments against the matching version documentation. Reduce a failing program to one query before rebuilding the loop.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Print intermediate values while debugging. Avoid catching every error without reporting it; a silent dashboard can be more misleading than an obvious stopped program."
  ],
  "task.1B00002670740DDB.title": "I completed the practical steps",
  "quest.77C10BE3D82578F8.title": "Project: a workshop status board",
  "quest.77C10BE3D82578F8.quest_subtitle": "Practical checklist",
  "quest.77C10BE3D82578F8.quest_desc": [
    "&6&lWHY&r",
    "A good first computer project saves you checking several interfaces while leaving production easy to control manually.",
    "",
    "&a&lDO&r",
    "Build a monitor showing at least two useful readings, such as a chest stock count and a supported colony or energy status. Label units, refresh with a delay, handle a missing device and preserve the working source.",
    "",
    "&e&lTIP&r",
    "Local examples are also saved beside this profile's guide documentation. Type them into the in-game editor or import them using your normal ComputerCraft file workflow."
  ]
}
